course: medicine
lesson: 9
title: Sore throats, ears and eyes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most sore throats are caused by viruses and get better on their own within about a week; ice lollies, cold drinks and painkillers help meanwhile.
explain: Behind your eardrum is a little space, the middle ear; after a cold it can fill with fluid, and the pressure makes the ear really hurt.
explain: Earwax is not dirt: the ear makes it to clean and protect itself, and cotton buds only push it deeper.
explain: Waking up with your eyes glued shut by yellow gunk is usually conjunctivitis, pink eye, which rarely harms eyesight and is cleaned gently with cooled boiled water.
explain: Itchy red eyes every spring are usually pollen allergy, which you cannot catch from anyone.
fact: Most sore throats are caused by viruses and get better within about a week; some are caused by streptococcus bacteria, and white spots on the tonsils can come from viruses as well as bacteria.
fact: Tonsils are lumps at the back of the throat that help fight germs; fewer tonsil operations are done now than in the past, and a few bouts of tonsillitis (sore, swollen tonsils) are not a reason to take them out.
fact: A middle ear infection fills the space behind the eardrum with fluid; most get better on their own in about three days, so painkillers are the main treatment; putting oil or anything else in the ear does not cure it.
fact: If the pressure gets very high the eardrum can get a tiny hole that lets pus out and eases the pain; it usually heals in a few weeks.
fact: Glue ear is sticky fluid left behind the eardrum after a cold that muffles sound; it often clears by itself, and if not, tiny tubes called grommets may help.
fact: Conjunctivitis makes the eye red, gritty and sticky; in the UK children don't have to stay off school with it.
fact: Allergic conjunctivitis from pollen is not catching and is helped by antihistamine (allergy medicine) drops or tablets.
fact: Swimmer's ear is an infection of the ear canal, outside the eardrum, when water stays in the ear; once it has healed, swimming can start again.
fact: Earwax cleans and protects the ear and usually moves out by itself; cotton buds push it deeper.
fact: A blow can burst a small blood vessel on the white of the eye; the red patch looks dramatic but usually heals in two weeks.
fact: The voice comes from two vocal cords; shouting can swell them so they cannot vibrate (shake fast) properly, and whispering can strain them too.
fact: A doctor looks inside the ear with an otoscope, a small torch with a magnifying lens.
exercise: What causes most sore throats? | Viruses | Eating ice cream | Shouting too much | Bacteria, every time
exercise: What are tonsils for? | Helping to fight germs | Helping you taste your food properly | Holding your tongue up at the back | Making your voice deeper as you grow
exercise: Do you need your tonsils out after a few bouts of tonsillitis? | No, not just for a few bouts | Yes, straight after the very first one | Yes, everyone has them out by ten | No, tonsils fall out on their own
exercise: Why does a middle ear infection hurt so much? | Fluid builds up behind the eardrum and presses | The ear is completely full of hard wax | A small bug has crawled in | The eardrum has slowly turned into hard bone
exercise: Yellow stuff came out of your ear and the pain stopped. What happened? | A tiny hole in the eardrum let the fluid out | The inside of the ear has melted | The brain is leaking out a little | The infection has spread right through the whole head
exercise: Will a small hole in the eardrum ruin your hearing for ever? | No, it usually heals itself in a few weeks | Yes, you will be deaf in that ear for life | Yes, unless you plug it with cotton wool | No, but the hole keeps growing bigger
exercise: Since your cold, everything sounds like underwater. What is it likely to be? | Glue ear, sticky fluid behind the eardrum | Too much earwax from the cold | A broken ear bone | Water you swallowed going up into your ears
exercise: You wake with eyes stuck together by yellow gunk. What's it probably called? | Conjunctivitis, pink eye | Hay fever from the bedroom | A black eye from rubbing | Glue ear spreading to the eyes
exercise: Your eyes go red and itchy every spring. What is it usually? | An allergy to pollen | A catching infection from school | Too much screen time in the holidays | A scratch on the eye from a nail
exercise: What is swimmer's ear? | An infection of the ear canal when water stays in | A tiny fish that has swum into your ear | Water that has got into your lungs | A broken eardrum from diving
exercise: Should you clean your ears with cotton buds? | No, they push wax deeper | Yes, every day, as deep as they go | Yes, because wax is just dirt | No, use a pencil end instead
exercise: You got hit in the eye and there's a bright red patch on the white. What usually happens? | It heals on its own in about two weeks | You will slowly lose the sight in that eye | It stays red for the rest of your life | It turns blue, then falls out
exercise: Why did you lose your voice after shouting at a match? | Your vocal cords swelled and can't vibrate properly | Your tongue got too tired to move | Your lungs ran out of air for ever | Your teeth have gone loose from all the shouting
exercise: Is whispering good for a lost voice? | No, it can strain the vocal cords too | Yes, always whisper until it's back | Yes, whispering heals the voice | No, you must shout to stretch it
exercise: What helps a sore throat while it gets better? | Painkillers, cold drinks and ice lollies | Hot spicy food to burn the germs | Shouting loudly to clear out the throat | Gargling with neat vinegar
exercise: What tool does a doctor use to look in your ear? | An otoscope, a small torch | A stethoscope on the chest | A thermometer under the tongue | A big round magnifying glass
test: ear's killing me, mate says pour olive oil in it to cure it. yeah? | No, putting oil in won't cure an ear infection | Yeah, the warm oil kills all the germs right in there | Yeah, hot oil works best | No, pour cold water in instead
test: My friend says pink eye means she'll go blind. Is she right? | No, conjunctivitis rarely harms eyesight | Yes, it always causes blindness | Yes, unless she wears glasses from now on | No, pink eye only affects the ears
test: Lily's eyes stream in spring, and her friend won't sit near her in case she catches it. Fair? | No, pollen allergy is not catching | Yes, it spreads just like a cold does | Yes, it spreads through the air in the classroom | No, but it spreads by touching her hands
test: What would happen if you poked a cotton bud deep into your ear every day? | Wax could be pushed deeper and block the ear | Your ears would stay perfectly clean | You would hear better and better each day, like a bat | Nothing at all
test: Why do doctors often wait before giving antibiotics for an earache? | Most ear infections get better on their own in about three days | Antibiotics make ears worse | Doctors are lazy | Ears can't be treated
test: White spots on my tonsils, so it's bacteria for sure? | Not for sure; viruses can cause them too | Yes, white spots always mean bacteria, no doubt | Yes, and you'll need an operation | No, it just means you had milk
test: Ben can't hear in class weeks after a cold. What might the doctor do? | Wait and check again, maybe grommets if it stays | Pull out his eardrum to let the fluid drain | Give him a new ear | Tell him to listen harder in class
test: In a cartoon a kid pulls a huge lump of wax from his ear and uses it as a candle. What's true about wax? | The ear makes it to clean and protect itself | Wax is just old dirt that gets stuck | Wax means you never wash | Wax should be dug out every single day with a pin
test: Your friend's eye is bright red after a football hit, but he can see fine and it doesn't hurt. Likely? | A burst small vessel that heals by itself | His eye is bleeding badly deep inside | He has caught pink eye from the ball | He needs glasses from now on
test: Got earache after the pool. Will I have to quit swimming for ever? | No, but the ear needs to heal first | Yes, for the rest of your life | Yes, until you're a grown-up | No, keep swimming through the ache
test: Which should you do for conjunctivitis at home? | Clean the eyes gently with cooled boiled water on cotton wool | Rub them hard with a towel | Rinse them with lemon juice to kill the germs | Stay off school for a month
exam: Identify the most frequent cause of sore throats in children. | Viral infection, which commonly resolves within one week | Bacterial infection invariably requiring a course of antibiotics | Consumption of cold confectionery | Excessive use of the voice alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises earwax? | A secretion which cleans and protects the ear canal | Accumulated dirt indicating poor hygiene of the ear | A substance to be removed daily with cotton buds | A sign of infection of the middle ear
exam: It is given that a child's eyes are red and itching each spring. State the most probable cause. | Allergy to pollen, which is not contagious | Bacterial infection transmissible to others in class | Excessive viewing of screens | Injury to the surface of the eyeball
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a small perforation of the eardrum consequent upon infection. | Such perforations usually heal spontaneously within weeks | Such perforations cause permanent and total deafness | Such perforations require the eardrum to be removed entirely | Such perforations increase in size indefinitely
